PART 9 — Important decisions that must not be compromised
There are a few things I want us to get right from the beginning.
1. One application, not a collection of scripts. All functionality must be accessible through the desktop application.
2. One central orchestrator, specialized agents. Avoid building three unrelated AI systems.
3. Independent MCP servers. Each server should be testable and usable independently.
4. Application adapters. New applications should be supported through extensions.
5. No compulsory vision model. Prefer APIs, accessibility, DOM, OCR and OpenCV. Use a VLM only as an optional future fallback if you ever decide to support one.
6. Persistent task execution. The agent must be able to restart and resume.
7. Real frontend functionality. No buttons that merely look operational.
8. Modular backend. Avoid placing the entire intelligence engine in one enormous Python file.
9. Model independence. Never tie the entire system to one LLM.
10. Security from the beginning. Do not postpone permission management until the final phase.
One further technical distinction: the MCP servers should not be the only way to control the computer. Some low-level operations are better handled by native Python services and operating-system APIs. The MCP layer should expose selected capabilities to the agents.

PART 10 — The final vision








aiproductivity.ai


When everything is finished, you should be able to open NEXUS AI and say:

"I want you to create a complete e-commerce application, organize its files, configure its database, test it and prepare it for deployment."

The system should:
- Understand your objective.
- Create a development plan.
- Generate TODOs.
- Select the coding agent.
- Inspect your computer.
- Create the workspace.
- Call the required MCP servers.
- Write the application.
- Configure the database.
- Run tests.
- Identify and correct errors.
- Verify the application.
- Save its progress.
- Present the finished result.
And later, when you simply say:

"Hey, how are you?"

Your companion should respond naturally, remember previous discussions and continue an appropriate conversation.

That is the product we are building: an autonomous personal AI operating environment delivered as a desktop application.

The initial target is your Arch Linux GNOME environment, with portability designed into the architecture rather than added as an afterthought. 
